    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "CHINESE NAME BE :\nWAj-LOONG\nTHE BRIDGE HOUSE HOTEL, LTD.\nTELEPHONE NO. 41687\nTELEGRAPHIC ADDRESS\n(LONG DISTANCE \u0026 LOCAL)\nBRIDGE, NANKING\nCODE: BENTLEYS PHRASE\n2/19 1935\nNanking,\nDearest Debo -\nHad a nees trip up thi\nmorning with Chuck Sharp.\nMr K. I. Nich evas a passengu\nand also Dr. Collins - our\nfrend the molar mauler-\nwho was going to tankoa.\n9 saur Mr Johnson and\nand mas\nReynolds ure out. Expret\nto see thim\nale again.\nExpect to spand a quiet\nnight at the hote -\nold fashioned - bit draft, but\nock wise \"fair\"\nMy eye is\nок.\nDid it hear from Dai",
      "CHINESE NAME BE * WAI-LOONG\nTHE BRIDGE HOUSE HOTEL, LID.\nTELEPHONE NO. 41687\n(LONG DISTANCE \u0026 LOCAL)\nTELEGRAPHIC ADDRESS\nBRIDGE. NANKING\nCODE:-BENTLEYS PHRASE\nNanking,\n193\ntoday so dont know\nwhether he is coming up\ntomorras or not.\nSo Hidin the Suredish\nExplore is here - he is\na very intrating Charactes\nYou no doukt had read\nof him - there was a\nhem gone editrial abont\ni this morang\nNo. China Dal, News. Also one\nabont Dr. Chu Chia -hua M of\nC.\nmust clon now - Expect me\nWhen yon Ost a radio that\nIm leaving.\nAll my lor\nIn crazy",
      "On February 23rd, 1935 at the Bridge House Hotel, Nanking,\nI spent a most interesting evening with Sven Hedin, who was in\nNanking for the purpose of reporting on an expedition from which he\nhad recently returned.\nThis expedition was financed by the Central Government\nand had as its obdective the mapping of a road through Western China\nfollowing the ancient silk trail from lanchow to Hami and Urumchi.\nIt was on this expedition that the famous Swedish explorer gathered\nthe material for a number of books which he later wrote dealing with\nconditions in the Chinese Northwest. It is interesting to note that\nfollowing the outbreak of the Sino-Japanese undeclared war, more than\na million men were put to work to complete this highway over which\nnow move Russian trucks carring munition and supplies to the Chinese\nforces.\nIf the Chinese lose Hankow, this highway will be one of\nthe principal roads used as a connection between China and the out-\nside world."
    ],
